__pycache__/
*.pyc
output/
.tmt_key
# Per-provider API keys, and the temporary file they are written through.
# Obfuscated, not encrypted: staying out of commits is half of what protects
# them, the file permissions being the other half.
.tmt_providers.json
.tmt_providers.json.tmp
.tmt_git.local
activatevenv.exe
logs/

# Build artefacts from an editable install. Regenerated by pip, never source.
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# The npm side. `npm install -g tmtcode` ships bin/ and scripts/ only, and the
# package has no dependencies -- so a node_modules here is somebody's local
# experiment and a .tgz is what `npm pack` leaves behind. The lock file is
# ignored for the same reason: there is nothing to lock.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
*.tgz

# Working references and per-install choices, not source.
DESIGN_PRINCIPLES.md
.tmt_model
.tmt_effort
# The commands the user has allowed or denied the bash tool, per workspace and
# globally. TMT's own state, and per-machine: a rule one person approved on
# their checkout is not a rule anyone else agreed to.
.tmt_bash_rules.json
.tmt_autoupdate
# Which tip the session header shows next. One integer, per installation, and
# meaningless to anyone else: it says how far through the catalogue THIS
# machine has read.
.tmt_tip
# Whether the persistent project context is on. TMT's own state, so it is
# ignored here beside the other per-install choices.
.tmt_context
# The web-search backend and its API key. A credential, so it is ignored for
# .tmt_providers.json's reason and not for .tmt_context's: staying out of
# commits is half of what protects it, the file permissions being the other
# half.
.tmt_search.json
# The file the key is written THROUGH. `os.replace` normally removes it in the
# same breath, but a failed move -- a read-only target, another process holding
# the file open, a crash between the write and the rename -- leaves it on disk
# with the key in clear text. `.tmt_providers.json.tmp` is ignored for exactly
# this reason and this one was not, which mattered the moment anything started
# writing it: when TMT is run on TMT the install and the workspace are the same
# directory, so it would appear in this repository's own git status.
.tmt_search.json.tmp
# And the copy kept when the store is found damaged. It holds whatever keys
# were in the file before it broke, in clear text, for exactly the same reason
# the two above do.
.tmt_search.json.damaged

# The context directory itself. In a project TMT is WORKING ON, these two
# markdown files are notes about that project, in that project, meant to be
# read and committed like any other documentation -- and nothing here changes
# that, because a .gitignore only governs the repository it sits in.
#
# This repository is the one place the rule does not pay. TMT's install and its
# workspace are the same directory when TMT is run on TMT, so every session
# driven here writes TMT's notes ABOUT TMT into TMT's own source tree, and they
# turn up in the status of a change that has nothing to do with them -- which
# is how they once ended up inside a commit that never mentioned them. They are
# the agent's working memory of this checkout, per-machine and regenerated on
# demand, so they belong beside .tmt_index/ and .tmt_memory/ rather than in the
# history.
TMT_Context/

# The code index and the project notebook. Both live beside the install, and
# when TMT is run ON TMT the install and the workspace are the same directory,
# so both land here and would otherwise be committed. Caches keyed by absolute
# path are worthless to anyone else and the notebook is per-machine.
.tmt_index/
.tmt_memory/
# The home the bash tool gives a command: HOME, TEMP and every package
# manager's cache point here, so npm, pip and cargo write their caches inside
# it instead of into the user's own. Same collision as the two above -- when
# TMT is run on TMT this is the workspace -- and the same answer.
.tmt_sandbox/

# Working notes for the assistant. Per-checkout, not source.
CLAUDE.local.md

# The publishing runbook: what the maintainer types to put this on npm, written
# for one person on one machine. It names PATH entries, a local clone and an
# npm account, so it is notes rather than documentation -- the README is where
# the install is documented for everybody else.
PUBLISHING.md

# Recon notes for the multi-agent work: machine-generated subsystem maps, kept
# beside CLAUDE.local.md as working material rather than source.
MULTIAGENT_RECON.md

# The brief for the next session on the multi-agent work. Working material.
multiagent_promt_*.md
